# The Keystone Intelligence Engine
## A Multi-Agent Architecture for Automated Consulting Research & Analysis

*Capstone Project — Econ Consulting (Prof. Youle) | Jack Riddle | IU Kelley School of Business | Spring 2026*

---

## 1. Executive Vision

Management consulting runs on research. Before a partner walks into a boardroom with a recommendation, dozens of hours have been spent pulling SEC filings, mapping competitive landscapes, sizing markets, stress-testing assumptions, and synthesizing it all into a narrative that drives a $50M decision. That research work — thorough, rigorous, expensive — is both the foundation of every engagement and the bottleneck that constrains how many engagements a firm can run at peak quality.

But research is also where the economics of knowledge work have fundamentally shifted. Across the consulting industry, 60–70% of billable hours are consumed not by the analytical work itself, but by the *coordination overhead* surrounding it — team alignment, task decomposition, methodology negotiation, intermediate review, deliverable formatting, and quality assurance (Jones, "Coordination Tax," Mar 12, 2026). When a three-analyst team spends a full week producing a competitive landscape analysis, the majority of that time is spent coordinating, not analyzing. The coordination tax is the invisible cost center of every consulting engagement.

This project builds an autonomous research engine that eliminates the coordination tax while dramatically expanding what a consultant can produce. The Keystone Intelligence Engine is a multi-agent LLM system that takes a research question — "Evaluate the competitive position of Company X in the autonomous vehicle sensor market" — and produces a complete, Keystone-quality analytical brief. Not a summary of Google results. Not a template with blanks. A grounded, source-cited, multi-perspective analysis with the depth and rigor a Senior Engagement Manager would expect from that three-analyst team working a full week.

The system does this through a six-layer pipeline architecture implementing DPVI — **Decompose-Parallelize-Verify-Iterate** — the convergent workflow pattern independently discovered by four organizations building production AI systems (Jones, "DPVI Pattern," Mar 11, 2026). The Specification Engine translates vague client questions into precise, executable research specifications. Parallel research agents fan out across public and proprietary data sources. A structured multi-perspective deliberation surfaces consensus, contested claims, and analytical gaps. An independent Evaluator — the most carefully engineered component in the system — grades every output through a five-layer evaluation stack anchored by a ten-dimension rubric calibrated to Keystone's own quality standards. And a self-improvement loop driven by an **Observation Library** turns every quality outcome (failures and successes) into a permanent architectural constraint, compounding the system's institutional intelligence with every engagement.

**The value proposition is capability expansion, not cost reduction.** The question isn't "Can Keystone do the same research with fewer analysts?" It's "What research would Keystone do if the cost dropped 10x?" (Jones, "Expansion Thesis," Mar 14, 2026). Enable research at a scope and depth that no human team could match in the available timeline. Turn one consultant with the Intelligence Engine into a team that can investigate 15–50 parallel research threads, run independent parallel analyses with structured aggregation, verify every claim against primary sources, and deliver Goldman-grade output in days instead of weeks — while simultaneously building the institutional memory that makes the 20th engagement meaningfully better than the 5th.

**What makes this a system, not a scaffold:** Most AI tools for professional services are scaffolds — well-structured templates that look impressive but can't pull a single number from an actual data source. The Hallucination Tax (2.5 hours of human verification for 30 seconds of AI generation) makes them net-negative on productivity for any analysis requiring factual accuracy (Prendergast, Wealth Management Plugin Analysis, 2025). This system is designed from the ground up as a *system*: connected to real data sources, with structural enforcement of source citation, automated verification of factual claims, and an evaluator that rejects unsourced assertions. Every number in the output is traceable to a source. The human reviewer's job is to evaluate the analysis, not to fact-check the data.

---

## 2. Architecture Overview

The original proposal described three layers: Data Ingestion, Content Structuring, and Generation/QA. After deep analysis of Anthropic's engineering practices, production multi-agent systems, the practitioner literature on agentic architectures, and the systematic findings from Nate Jones's 91-article corpus on AI system design (Dec 2025 – Mar 2026), that design evolves into a six-layer architecture with a meta-optimization loop, organized around two foundational principles:

1. **The specification layer IS the system.** The model improves automatically. The tools improve automatically. The specification layer — human-authored definitions of what should be built, for whom, to what standard — improves only through deliberate investment. The .md files that define research methodology, quality criteria, and analytical frameworks are the only irreplaceable component (Jones, "Markdown-as-Specification," Mar 27, 2026).

2. **Structure determines outcomes more than capability does.** Any system whose quality depends on agents *choosing* to comply with instructions will eventually fail. Quality must be enforced architecturally — through tool design, pipeline structure, and handoff contracts — not through prompt instructions that drift approximately 40% of the time (@jordymaui, "Your OpenClaw is Ignoring Half Your Instructions").

```
